# Automation stack secrets (services VM)
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/automation.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys —
# the stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting — the decrypt-and-source path expands it
# otherwise. (None of the generate commands below emit '$'.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Home Assistant owner account, created headlessly by automation-init
# (POST /api/onboarding/users, which works exactly once).
#
# HA_ADMIN_NAME is a display name; keep it a single token — a space would need
# quoting to survive both the decrypt-and-source path and compose's env_file
# parsing, and it is not worth it for a label.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HA_ADMIN_NAME=Idan
HA_ADMIN_USER=idan
HA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MQTT credentials. mosquitto-init hashes all three into the broker's password
# file at every start (with -H sha512-pbkdf2 pinned explicitly, so the file
# stays readable by both mosquitto 2.0 and 2.1), and rotating any of them here
# and redeploying is what propagates it.
#
# FRIGATE_MQTT_PASSWORD carries that prefix on purpose: Frigate substitutes
# only FRIGATE_-prefixed variables into its config, so one key serves both the
# broker's password file and Frigate's `{FRIGATE_MQTT_PASSWORD}` reference.
# Renaming it breaks Frigate's MQTT connection silently — the substitution
# leaves the literal text in place.
#
# The healthcheck account exists because `allow_anonymous false` leaves no
# credential-free way to probe the broker.
# Generate each: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FRIGATE_MQTT_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
MQTT_HA_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
MQTT_HEALTHCHECK_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frigate cameras — LEAVE EMPTY until you have cameras.
#
# Empty is a complete, working state: Frigate explicitly supports `cameras: {}`
# (it is the config it writes for itself on first run), so the stack deploys
# and runs as an NVR with nothing to record.
#
# To add one: put its full RTSP URL — credentials included, which is why this
# lives here — in a FRIGATE_CAM_<NAME> key, then reference it from
# frigate.yaml as "{FRIGATE_CAM_<NAME>}" under the camera's ffmpeg inputs.
# Add the key to this file, the real .sops.env AND tests/fixtures/
# automation.sops.env together, or the drift lint fails.
#
# Two things that change with the camera set: shm_size (the compose file
# carries the per-camera formula) and the retention days in frigate.yaml.
#
# ⚠ A literal { or } inside one of these URLs raises during config parsing —
# and Frigate does not crash on that, it starts in SAFE MODE with no cameras
# and MQTT off, looking perfectly healthy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FRIGATE_CAM_EXAMPLE=
